    <!-- Mathematical Formulation of Host Issue Bound -->
    <div class="callout callout-warning" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
      <div class="callout-header">
        <span class="badge badge-accent">MATHEMATICAL FORMULATION</span>
        <h3 class="callout-title">The Scalar Issue Bottleneck Theorem</h3>
      </div>
      <p>
        The execution time <em>T</em><sub>vector</sub> of any vector loop on a coprocessor coupled to an in-order scalar host is governed by the maximum of the spatial execution datapath latency and the host scalar instruction-dispatch latency:
      </p>
      <div style="background: #151b20; padding: 1rem 1.5rem; border-radius: 6px; border: 1px solid #334155; margin: 1rem 0; font-family: monospace; color: #f8fafc; font-size: 0.95rem; text-align: center;">
        T<sub>vector</sub> = max &lpar; N / (Lanes &times; FMA<sub>per_lane</sub>) , N<sub>scalar_instr</sub> / IssueRate<sub>host</sub> &rpar;
      </div>
      <p>
        In dense GEMM, a single <code>vfmacc.vv</code> amortizes over <em>VLEN</em> / 64 operations across 4 to 16 lanes, making <em>T</em><sub>vector</sub> purely datapath-bound. However, in Softmax or FFT, each vector arithmetic instruction requires auxiliary scalar setup (exponent scalar loops, pointer offsets, shift amounts). Because scalar host issue rate is strictly bounded at 1 instruction/cycle, the coprocessor's lanes sit idle 77% of the time waiting for the next dispatch.
      </p>
    </div>
